        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Water-Based vs Solvent vs Two-Part vs Epoxy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water-based fillers, often labeled latex or acrylic, are the most common and the easiest to use. They have low odor, clean up with water, sand easily and take paint well. Their weaknesses are shrinkage on deep fills, slower drying in thick sections, and limited strength. They are the right choice for nail holes, small gouges, cracks in trim and most interior work, and the better ones are rated for exterior use as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solvent-based fillers, like the classic versions of Plastic Wood and FamoWood, dry harder and faster than most water-based fillers and have long been favored by cabinet shops. They also smell strongly, need solvent for cleanup and can dry out in the can. Most brands now offer latex versions that handle similarly, which is why the latex DAP Plastic Wood-X and FamoWood Latex appear on this list rather than their solvent-based siblings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two-part polyester fillers, such as Minwax High Performance, cure chemically when you mix in a hardener. They do not shrink, they harden in minutes regardless of depth, and they resist water well, which makes them good for exterior repairs that will be painted. They are hard to sand evenly against soft wood, have a short working time, and do not take stain like wood.
          </p>

