        <div id="filler-vs-putty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Wood Filler vs Wood Putty vs Spackle vs Caulk
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wood filler and wood putty are not the same thing, and the difference determines when you use each. Filler hardens completely and is applied to bare wood before finishing; you sand it flush and then stain or paint over it. Putty stays slightly pliable, usually comes pre-colored, and is pressed into nail holes after the wood is already stained and finished, because it does not need sanding and would gum up if you tried. Our guide to <a href="/spackle-vs-putty/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">spackle vs putty</a> goes deeper on where each belongs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spackle is for drywall and plaster. It sands beautifully and is the right product for nail holes in painted walls, but it is soft, absorbs water and has no business on exterior wood or in anything that flexes. On painted interior trim, a lightweight spackle can stand in for wood filler on small nail holes, but it will not hold a screw or survive a bump. If you are not sure which compound suits a wall repair, our comparison of <a href="/joint-compound-vs-spackle/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">joint compound vs spackle</a> and our roundup of the <a href="/best-spackle/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best spackle</a> cover the drywall side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Caulk is for joints that move. The gap where trim meets a wall, where a window casing meets siding, or between two boards that expand and contract at different rates should be caulked, not filled. Wood filler is rigid, and a rigid filler in a moving joint cracks within a season. The rule is simple: fill holes and defects within a board, caulk the joints between materials.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where the categories overlap, choose by finish. If the wood will be painted, a paintable latex filler or even a high-quality caulk in a small joint is fine. If it will be stained, use a stainable filler before finishing or a color-matched putty after finishing. If it is outdoors and structural, use epoxy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stainable" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Stainable vs Paintable: The Honest Truth
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every wood filler on the shelf says stainable. In practice, that means the filler accepts stain without repelling it, not that it will look like the surrounding wood afterward. Wood absorbs stain unevenly through its pores and grain, creating the variation that makes it look like wood. Filler is a uniform paste with no grain, so it absorbs stain uniformly and reads as a flat, solid-colored patch, often lighter or darker than the board around it.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are three ways to get a stained repair that disappears. The first is to start with a filler that is already close to the final color, such as a species-colored filler, or to tint a water-based filler with the same stain or dye before you use it. The second is to fill with a stainable filler, stain the whole piece, and then touch up the patch with a fine brush and a slightly darker stain or a graining pen to fake the grain. The third, for small nail holes, is to finish the wood first and then fill with color-matched putty.
          </p>

          <p>
            Always test on a scrap of the same wood. Fill a hole in an offcut, let it dry fully, sand it as you will on the project and apply the same stain and topcoat. Five minutes of testing saves you from discovering a pale patch in the middle of a tabletop after the finish is on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paint is far more forgiving. Any properly cured filler will disappear under paint, provided the repair is sanded flush and primed. Fillers, especially two-part and epoxy types, absorb paint differently from bare wood, so a coat of primer over the repair keeps the topcoat sheen uniform. Our guide to the <a href="/best-primer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best primer</a> explains which primers suit bare wood and filler, and for exterior surfaces such as decks and porches the <a href="/best-deck-paint/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best deck paint</a> picks cover the topcoat.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Tint first, stain second.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If a repair has to vanish under stain, color the filler to match before you use it. Relying on stain to color a neutral filler afterward almost never produces an invisible repair.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="exterior" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Interior vs Exterior Repairs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Exterior wood faces water, sun, freezing and the constant swelling and shrinking of boards as humidity changes. A filler that is rigid, brittle or water-sensitive will crack, pop out or hold water against the wood, which accelerates the very rot you were trying to fix. That is why interior-only fillers, such as Goodfilla, should never go outdoors, and why even exterior-rated latex fillers belong in small holes rather than large structural repairs.
          </p>

          <p>
            For outdoor nail holes, small dents and minor cracks in trim, siding and fences, an exterior-rated latex filler like DAP Plastic Wood-X or Gorilla is fine, provided it is primed and painted. Paint is what protects the repair; an unpainted latex filler outdoors will weather away. For anything larger, or anywhere water sits, such as window sills, the bottoms of door casings, deck rail caps and the ends of boards, use a two-part polyester or epoxy filler.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not use wood filler to close gaps between exterior boards or between trim and siding. Those joints move with the seasons and belong to caulk. Our guides to the <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best exterior caulk</a> and the <a href="/best-caulk-for-wood-siding/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best caulk for wood siding</a> cover which sealants flex enough for that job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing matters outdoors. Most fillers need moderate temperatures to dry or cure properly, and epoxies in particular cure slowly in the cold. Abatron's standard WoodEpox wants 50 to 90 degrees F. Plan exterior repairs for dry weather when the wood itself is dry, because filler applied over damp wood will not bond well and can trap moisture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rot" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Repairing Rotted Wood with Epoxy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rot repair is where a good filler saves real money. Replacing a rotted window sill, door jamb or piece of historic molding can mean removing trim, paying a carpenter and repainting a whole wall. An epoxy repair, done properly, can be as durable as new wood for a fraction of the cost, and it preserves original material on older homes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start by removing everything that is soft. Dig out rotted wood with a chisel, awl or carbide scraper until you reach firm wood, then let the area dry thoroughly. Epoxy will not bond to wet or rotten fibers, and sealing moisture into the wood guarantees the rot continues underneath. A <a href="/best-moisture-meter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">moisture meter</a> takes the guesswork out of deciding whether the remaining wood is dry enough to repair.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, consolidate. A thin, penetrating epoxy such as Abatron's LiquidWood soaks into the slightly soft wood at the edges of the repair and hardens it, giving the filler a solid base to bond to. Brush on several coats until the wood stops absorbing it. Then press in the epoxy filler while the consolidant is still tacky, overfilling slightly, and shape it roughly with a putty knife before it hardens.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once cured, shape the repair with a rasp, plane or sander to match the original profile, then prime and paint. Epoxy is not UV stable on its own, so paint is not optional outdoors. Finally, fix the cause. Rot almost always comes from water that sits on or runs into the wood, so improve the slope of the sill, caulk the joint above it or redirect the downspout, or the repair will simply outlast the wood around it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Apply Wood Filler So It Disappears
          </h2>

          <p>
            Prepare the surface first. Clean out loose fibers, dust, old finish and any wax or oil from the hole, because filler bonds poorly to contaminated wood. On bare wood, a clean, dry surface is enough. On previously finished wood, scuff the area around the repair so the filler has something to grip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Press the filler in firmly with a flexible putty knife, working it into the hole rather than smearing it over the top. Overfill slightly, so there is material to sand back to flush, but keep the excess confined to the repair; filler smeared onto the surrounding wood seals the pores and shows up as a light halo under stain. For deep holes with water-based filler, build in layers of about 1/4 inch and let each dry fully before adding the next.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let it dry or cure completely. Water-based fillers dry from the outside in, so a repair can feel hard on top while the core is soft. Follow the drying times on the label, extend them in cool or humid conditions, and use indicators like DAP's DryDex color change when available. Sanding too soon tears the filler out of the hole.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sand flush with a sanding block, starting at 120 or 150 grit and finishing at 180 or 220 to match the rest of the surface. A block keeps the repair flat; sanding with fingers alone dishes the soft wood around a hard filler. On larger areas, a <a href="/best-palm-sander/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">palm sander</a> speeds things up, but go gently on soft woods. Wipe off the dust, check the repair under raking light, and fill any pinholes before finishing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="woodworking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Grain Filling and Furniture Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            In furniture making, filler does two jobs. The first is fixing defects, such as a knot hole, a tear-out or a gap in a joint. The second is grain filling: packing the open pores of woods like oak, ash, walnut and mahogany so a film finish can build to a smooth, glassy surface. Grain filler is usually thinner than hole filler, applied across the grain and then scraped off, leaving the pores filled.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based products like Goodfilla handle both jobs because they can be thinned and tinted. Tinting the grain filler slightly darker than the wood emphasizes the grain pattern; matching it makes the pores disappear. Either way, test on scrap, because the color of a filler changes once finish goes over it.
          </p>

          <p>
            For defects on furniture, color matching matters more than strength. Tint the filler to the final finished color before filling, or use an epoxy and plan to paint, or finish first and fill nail holes with a colored putty. For gaps in joints, a filler is a cosmetic fix only; a joint that has opened up because it failed needs re-gluing, not filling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Accurate cuts reduce how much filler you need in the first place. Tight miters on trim and picture frames hide far better than gaps packed with filler, so if you are fighting open corners on every project, the problem may be the saw. Our new guide to the <a href="/best-miter-saw/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best miter saw</a> covers the saws that cut clean, tight miters.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When Not to Use Wood Filler
          </h2>

          <p>
            Do not use filler on wood that is still moving. Green or wet lumber shrinks as it dries and will push filler out of cracks or pull it apart. Let the wood reach equilibrium with its surroundings first, especially for indoor furniture made from freshly bought boards.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not use filler where a structural repair is needed. Latex filler in a cracked stair tread, a split joist or a broken chair leg adds no strength. Glue and clamp splits that can be closed, reinforce broken parts with dowels or screws, and reserve epoxy for situations where it truly bonds and carries load, such as a rebuilt corner or a stripped screw hole.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not fill large gaps in floors with wood filler. Gaps between floorboards open and close seasonally, and rigid filler cracks out and ends up in the vacuum. Small nail holes in flooring are fine to fill; wide seasonal gaps are better left alone or fixed with flooring-specific products or wood strips glued to one board.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, if the repair is larger than the board it sits in, replace the board. A deck board, fence picket or length of baseboard is often cheaper and faster to replace than to rebuild with filler, and the result will look better. Filler is for defects; it is not a substitute for new wood when most of the wood is gone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the word stainable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stainable means the filler accepts stain, not that it will match the wood. Filler has no grain, so it stains as a flat patch. Test on a scrap first, and for repairs that must disappear, tint a water-based filler to the finished color or fill after finishing with color-matched putty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling a deep hole in one pass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water-based fillers dry from the surface inward, so a thick fill skins over while the core stays soft, then shrinks and cracks. Build deep repairs in layers of about 1/4 inch, letting each dry, or use a two-part filler or epoxy that cures chemically regardless of depth.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using filler in joints that move</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gaps between trim and walls, siding and casings, or neighboring floorboards open and close with the seasons. Rigid filler cracks out of them within months and has to be dug out again. Fill holes and defects inside a board, and seal joints between boards or materials with a flexible caulk instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Patching over rot without digging it out</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Filler packed over soft, rotted wood traps moisture and lets decay continue underneath, so the patch fails and the damage grows. Remove all soft wood, let the area dry completely, harden the edges with an epoxy consolidant, then fill with epoxy, shape it and paint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving unpainted filler outdoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even exterior-rated latex fillers rely on paint to protect them from water and sun. Left bare, they weather, absorb moisture and loosen. Prime and paint every exterior repair promptly, and use epoxy, which also needs paint for UV protection, wherever water sits on the wood.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best wood filler for exterior use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For small holes and cracks in painted exterior trim, an exterior-rated latex filler such as DAP Plastic Wood-X works well if it is primed and painted. For window sills, door bottoms and anywhere water sits, use a two-part filler like Minwax High Performance or an epoxy such as Abatron WoodEpox, which do not shrink and resist water and rot.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is wood filler the same as wood putty?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Wood filler hardens fully and is applied to bare wood before finishing, then sanded flush and stained or painted. Wood putty stays slightly soft, usually comes pre-colored, and is pressed into nail holes after the wood has been stained and finished. Use filler during the build and putty for touch-ups on finished surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you stain wood filler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most fillers labeled stainable will accept stain, but none will look exactly like wood, because filler has no grain and absorbs stain evenly. The result is usually a flat patch that is lighter or darker than the board. For the best match, tint the filler before use, test on scrap, or fill finished wood with color-matched putty.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does wood filler take to dry?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It varies widely. Fast latex fillers like FamoWood can dry in about 15 minutes on shallow repairs, while DAP Plastic Wood-X quotes 3 to 6 hours for fills up to 1/8 inch and 36 hours for deeper ones. Two-part fillers and epoxies cure chemically, often sandable within 30 minutes to a couple of hours regardless of depth.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I use to fill large holes in wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For large holes, use a two-part epoxy or polyester filler, which cures without shrinking regardless of depth. Water-based fillers can work if you build the repair in thin layers and let each dry, but they shrink and take far longer. If the damaged area is bigger than the board itself, replacing the board is usually the better fix.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I screw into wood filler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Into most cured epoxy, yes. J-B Weld says KwikWood can be drilled and tapped after an hour, and Minwax High Performance filler can anchor screws. Some latex fillers, including DAP Plastic Wood-X and Gorilla, also claim to hold screws or nails, but for a hinge or anything under load, an epoxy repair or a glued-in wood plug is far more reliable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use wood filler or caulk on trim?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use wood filler for nail holes, dents and gouges within the trim itself, and caulk for the joints where trim meets the wall, ceiling or another piece of trim. Those joints move slightly with temperature and humidity, and only a flexible caulk will stay sealed. Rigid filler in a moving joint cracks out within a season.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

